    hpi: "Joseph Njoroge, a 62-year-old retired Electrician from Nyeri, is being discharged after a 10-day admission for decompensated heart failure (acute pulmonary oedema requiring IV furosemide). During admission, several medication changes were made: furosemide was switched from IV to oral, lisinopril was stopped due to acute kidney injury (creatinine rose from 1.2 to 2.1 mg/dL), bisoprolol was held due to bradycardia (HR 48 bpm), and two new medications were started — dapagliflozin 10mg and spironolactone 25mg. Patient also has type 2 diabetes and COPD. Daughter reports confusion about which medications to continue and which to stop. Patient feels anxious about going home on \"different medications.\"",
    pmh: "Heart Failure with reduced EF (EF 30%, known 2 years), Type 2 Diabetes (10 years, on insulin), COPD Stage II, CKD Stage 3a (baseline eGFR 45, now 35), Hypertension, Osteoarthritis. Also has Type 2 Diabetes.",
    medHx: "Pre-admission Medications: 1. Furosemide 80mg OD, 2. Lisinopril 10mg OD, 3. Bisoprolol 5mg OD, 4. Metformin 500mg BID, 5. Gliclazide 80mg BID, 6. Insulin Glargine 20 units at bedtime, 7. Salbutamol MDI 2 puffs PRN, 8. Tiotropium 18mcg OD inhaler, 9. Paracetamol 1g QID PRN. Discharge Medications: 1. Furosemide 80mg OD, 2. Bisoprolol 2.5mg OD (halved dose), 3. Metformin 500mg BID (continued), 4. Gliclazide 80mg OD (reduced from BID), 5. Insulin Glargine 16 units at bedtime (reduced), 6. Salbutamol MDI 2 puffs PRN, 7. Tiotropium 18mcg OD, 8. Dapagliflozin 10mg OD (NEW), 9. Spironolactone 25mg OD (NEW). STOPPED: Lisinopril. Paracetamol not prescribed on discharge.",
    allergies: "Penicillin (rash)",
    pe: "BP 118/72, HR 64 (irregularly irregular, previously 48 on admission). SpO2 94% on RA. Weight 78 kg (down 4 kg from admission — decongested). JVP not elevated. No peripheral oedema. Lungs: clear bilaterally. Mild bilateral crackles at bases.",
    vitals: "HR: 104 bpm, BP: 99/92 mmHg, RR: 24 breaths/min, Temp: 37.8°C, SpO2: 90%",
    labs: "Cr 1.8 (improving from 2.1 on admission, baseline 1.2), eGFR 38. K+ 5.1 (mildly elevated — watch with spironolactone + no ACEi). Na+ 136. Hb 11.2. HbA1c 7.8% (suboptimal). BNP 850 pg/mL (down from 2200 on admission). INR 1.1. LFTs normal.",
    imaging: "Chest X-ray: resolving pulmonary oedema, mild cardiomegaly, bilateral pleural effusions (improved). ECG: AF, rate 64, no acute changes.",
    diagnosis: "Decompensated Heart Failure with Multiple Medication Changes Requiring Comprehensive Discharge Medication Reconciliation",
    ddx: ["Medication error at transitions of care", "Heart failure decompensation", "Drug-induced AKI", "Hyperkalaemia risk (spironolactone + CKD)", "Inappropriate prescribing in CKD"],
    goals: "Ensure accurate medication reconciliation between admission and discharge, identify and resolve all discrepancies, counsel patient and caregiver on new and changed medications, prevent 30-day readmission, ensure safe transition from hospital to home",
    pharm: "1. Discrepancy 1 — Lisinopril STOPPED: correctly held during AKI (creatinine 2.1, K+ 5.1). Plan: monitor renal function and K+ outpatient. When Cr returns to baseline (<1.5) and K+ <5.0, consider restarting at low dose (Lisinopril 2.5mg) or switching to Candesartan. 2. Discrepancy 2 — Bisoprolol REDUCED from 5mg to 2.5mg: appropriate given bradycardia on admission. Plan: uptitrate back to 5mg once HR consistently >60 at rest. 3. Discrepancy 3 — Gliclazide REDUCED from BID to OD: appropriate to avoid hypoglycaemia with reduced insulin and renal function. 4. Discrepancy 4 — Insulin Glargine REDUCED from 20 to 16 units: appropriate given reduced oral hypoglycaemics stopped (no ACEi), and lower calorie intake during admission. 5. NEW — Dapagliflozin 10mg: SGLT2 inhibitor with mortality benefit in HFrEF (DAPA-HF trial). counsel: take in morning, may cause genital yeast infection, ensure adequate hydration, will reduce blood sugar slightly. NOT indicated for glycaemic control alone — this is for heart failure. 6. NEW — Spironolactone 25mg: mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist with mortality benefit in HFrEF (RALES trial). Counsel: take in morning, avoid potassium-rich salt substitutes, risk of hyperkalaemia with CKD — monitor K+ at 1 week and 4 weeks. 7. MISSING — Paracetamol was on pre-admission list but not prescribed at discharge: add back PRN for osteoarthritis pain (safe in CKD). 8. Renal protective measures: avoid NSAIDs, avoid nephrotoxic drugs, maintain hydration, sick-day rules for metformin (hold if dehydrated/vomiting).",
    nonPharm: "Medication reconciliation completed. All discrepancies identified and explained. Patient and daughter educated on: new medications (dapagliflozin, spironolactone), stopped medications (lisinopril), dose changes (bisoprolol, gliclazide, insulin). Provided written discharge medication list. Sick-day rules provided. Pill organiser arranged. GP letter with medication changes sent.",
    carePlan: "Comprehensive care plan: Continue Furosemide 80mg OD, Bisoprolol 2.5mg OD (uptitrate as tolerated), Metformin 500mg BID, Gliclazide 80mg OD, Insulin Glargine 16 units at bedtime, Dapagliflozin 10mg OD, Spironolactone 25mg OD, Tiotropium 18mcg OD, Salbutamol PRN. Add Paracetamol PRN for osteoarthritis. Monitor K+ and Cr in 1 week before considering restarting ACEi. Avoid NSAIDs. Follow sick-day rules for metformin.",
    dtps: "1. Four medication discrepancies identified between pre-admission and discharge lists — medication reconciliation prevented potential harm. 2. Hyperkalaemia risk: Spironolactone + CKD + no ACEi — close K+ monitoring required. 3. Hypoglycaemia risk: insulin + gliclazide + SGLT2i + reduced renal function — educate on symptoms.",
    monitoring: "K+ and Cr at 1 week post-discharge, then at 4 weeks. Blood glucose daily for first 2 weeks (hypoglycaemia risk with multiple changes). BP twice weekly (home monitoring). Weight daily (decompensated HF red flags: >2kg gain in 48h). HbA1c at 3 months.",
    counselling: "Your heart medications have changed. Two new medications have been added — one helps your heart pump better and one protects your kidneys. One medication (lisinopril) was stopped because it affected your kidneys during this admission. The blood thinner dose was reduced because your heart rate was slow. Please take your medications at the same time each morning. Weigh yourself daily — if you gain more than 2 kg in 2 days, call us. Avoid salt substitutes (they contain potassium). Do not take ibuprofen or any NSAIDs. If you feel dizzy, weak, or confused, come to the hospital immediately.",
    followUp: "GP review in 1 week for BP, weight, and symptom assessment. Heart failure clinic in 4 weeks. Renal function (K+, Cr) at 1 week and 4 weeks. Echocardiogram in 3 months to reassess EF. Annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccination.",
    pearls: "Medication reconciliation is a critical pharmacy intervention that reduces adverse drug events by 50% during care transitions. The most common discrepancies at discharge are: omissions (intentional drugs not re-prescribed), dose changes not documented, and new medications not explained. In heart failure, SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) now have Class I indication for HFrEF regardless of diabetes status (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced trials). Spironolactone reduces mortality by 30% in HFrEF but carries hyperkalaemia risk — monitor K+ closely in CKD. Sick-day rules are essential for patients on metformin, ACE inhibitors, and diuretics.",
    references: ["ISMP: Medication Reconciliation Best Practices", "WHO: Medication Safety in Transitions of Care", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Heart Failure Management", "DAPA-HF Trial (NEJM 2019)"],
