    counselling: "Vancomycin is a powerful antibiotic, but it must be carefully dosed to make sure enough drug reaches the bacteria. We are increasing your dose based on blood levels. We need frequent blood tests to monitor kidney function and drug levels. The infected central line must be removed to fully treat the infection — this is a critical step. Drink plenty of fluids. Report any hearing changes, ringing in ears, or dizziness (vancomycin can rarely affect hearing).",
    followUp: "Infectious Diseases follow-up for duration of therapy. Repeat echocardiogram to rule out endocarditis if persistent bacteraemia. Continue trough monitoring q72h. If renal function stabilises, can transition to outpatient parenteral antimicrobial therapy (OPAT). Audiological assessment if therapy extends beyond 4 weeks.",
    pearls: "Vancomycin pharmacokinetics: Volume of distribution = 0.7 L/kg (approx). Half-life: 6-12 hours with normal renal function, extends significantly in AKI. Trough monitoring is standard but AUC-guided dosing (AUC24/MIC >400) is superior for efficacy and safety. The ratio of vancomycin to piperacillin-tazobactam in causing AKI is synergistic — avoid this combination when possible. MRSA with vancomycin MIC >1.5 mcg/mL should be treated with alternative agents (daptomycin, linezolid, ceftaroline). Red Man Syndrome is not an allergic reaction — it is a histamine release phenomenon that can be managed by slowing the infusion and pre-treating with antihistamines. Remove the central line — antibiotics without source control will NOT cure the infection.",
